# 12. SAVED SUMMARIES AND MEMORY

The mentor should be able to retain useful review history locally without sending all historical data to the provider.

### Saved report types

Support locally saved:
- Daily debriefs
- Weekly reviews
- Monthly reviews

Each saved report should include:
- Stable ID
- Report type
- Date range
- Generated timestamp
- Relevant metric snapshot, when useful
- AI-generated report text
- Context categories used
- Whether journal text was included

Do not include full private journal content in the report metadata.

### Persistence rules

- Store saved reports in the existing local-first architecture.
- Reuse existing storage utilities.
- Do not overwrite a previous report without confirmation.
- Allow the user to view, rename if appropriate, and delete saved reports.
- Make the source date range clear.
- Mark reports as historical snapshots so they are not mistaken for current live metrics.

### Long-term pattern detection

Use saved reports and available history to help identify longer-term patterns.

However:
- Do not claim persistent AI memory unless the application actually implements it.
- Do not automatically send all saved reports with each request.
- Allow the user to select which historical reports are included.
- Prefer compact factual summaries over entire conversations.
- Clearly distinguish historical observations from current records.
- Let the user delete locally stored AI reports and conversation history.

Do not create a second source of truth for study, fitness, habit, or planning data.

